# Selecto Other Joins Workbook

## Setup 1) Install Dependencies

Run this cell first. It installs the local `selecto_livebooks` project and its
current Selecto dependencies for this Livebook session.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install!()
```

## Overview

This workbook covers join patterns beyond basic dot-notation selection:

1. Enabling a configured join with a different join type
2. Parameterized joins (`join_parameterize/4`) for multiple aliases of the same relationship
3. Subquery joins (`join_subquery/4`) for joining derived result sets
4. Custom dynamic joins (`join/3`) to tables not declared as associations in the source schema
5. Structured shaped output for advanced joins via `select_shape/2`

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  base[Configure base query]
  mode[Choose join strategy]
  select[Select join fields]
  filter[Apply filters]
  sql[Inspect SQL and params]
  run[Execute]

  base --> mode --> select --> filter --> sql --> run
```

```mermaid
graph LR
  orders[orders]
  customers[customers]
  order_items[order_items]
  subq[high_value_order_subquery]

  orders -->|customer_id to id| customers
  orders -->|id to order_id| order_items
  customers -->|id to customer_id| subq
```

Before running, initialize the sample database:

```bash
mix setup
```

## Setup 2) Connect to Shared Repo and Build Domains

This cell starts the shared sample-app repo, loads maintained domains from
`selecto_livebooks`, and adds the customer join filter metadata needed for the
parameterized join examples.

```elixir
base_config = SelectoLivebooks.NotebookSupport.config([:order_domain, :customer_domain, :order_items_domain])

order_domain =
  put_in(base_config.order_domain, [:joins, :customer, :filters], %{
    "tier" => %{type: "string"},
    "active" => %{type: "boolean"}
  })

config = %{base_config | order_domain: order_domain}

counts = SelectoLivebooks.NotebookSupport.table_counts(["orders", "customers", "order_items"])
order_count = counts["orders"]
customer_count = counts["customers"]
item_count = counts["order_items"]

IO.puts("Connected. Orders: #{order_count}, Customers: #{customer_count}, Order items: #{item_count}")

config
```

## Setup 3) Define Join Helpers

This helper cell prints SQL/params and executes a query with a compact preview.

```elixir
defmodule JoinLab.Helpers do
  @moduledoc false

  def explain(label, query), do: Selecto.Livebook.explain(label, query)

  def run(label, query, preview_count \\ 10) do
    Selecto.Livebook.run(label, query, preview_count: preview_count)
  end

  def run_shape(label, query, preview_count \\ 10) do
    explain(label, query)
    IO.inspect(query.set.selection_shape, label: "Compiled shape plan", pretty: true, limit: :infinity)

    case Selecto.execute_shape(query) do
      {:ok, shaped_rows} = ok ->
        IO.puts("Shaped rows: #{length(shaped_rows)}")
        IO.inspect(Enum.take(shaped_rows, preview_count), label: "Shape preview (up to #{preview_count} rows)")
        ok

      {:error, error} = failure ->
        IO.puts("Error: #{inspect(error)}")
        failure
    end
  end
end
```

## Setup 4) Current Expression DSL Imports

This workbook still keeps explicit string selection where parameterized alias
names make that clearer, but ordinary filters, orders, and non-aliased joins now
use the shipped expression DSL where it reads better.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

alias Selecto.Expr, as: X

:ok
```

## 1) Join Type Override on a Configured Join

The `customer` join is configured as `:left` in the domain. This cell enables that same join at runtime and overrides it to `:inner`.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

inner_customer_join_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.join(:customer, type: :inner)
  |> Selecto.select(select([order_number, status, total, customer.name, customer.tier, customer.country]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(customer.active == true))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JoinLab.Helpers.run("Configured join with runtime type override", inner_customer_join_query)
```

## 2) Parameterized Join Aliases (`join_parameterize/4`)

This creates two aliases of the same `customer` join (`customer:premium` and `customer:standard`) with different join filters.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

parameterized_join_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.join_parameterize(:customer, "premium", tier: "premium")
  |> Selecto.join_parameterize(:customer, "standard", tier: "standard")
  |> Selecto.select([
    "order_number",
    "status",
    "total",
    "customer:premium.name",
    "customer:standard.name"
  ])
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JoinLab.Helpers.run("Parameterized joins with tier filters", parameterized_join_query)
```

## 2.1) Parameterized Joins with `select_shape/2`

Render parameterized join results in a nested row shape so premium and standard alias fields are grouped explicitly.

```elixir
parameterized_shape_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.join_parameterize(:customer, "premium", tier: "premium")
  |> Selecto.join_parameterize(:customer, "standard", tier: "standard")
  |> Selecto.select_shape([
    "order_number",
    {"status", "total"},
    {"customer:premium.name", "customer:standard.name"}
  ])
  |> Selecto.order_by({"total", :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

{:ok, shaped_rows} =
  JoinLab.Helpers.run_shape("Parameterized joins with select_shape", parameterized_shape_query)

Enum.each(shaped_rows, fn [order_number, {status, total}, {premium_name, standard_name}] ->
  IO.puts(
    "order=#{order_number} status=#{status} total=#{inspect(total)} " <>
      "premium=#{inspect(premium_name)} standard=#{inspect(standard_name)}"
  )
end)

:ok
```

## 3) Parameterized Join with Boolean Filter Variants

You can also parameterize by boolean filters and compare active vs inactive customer matches side by side.

```elixir
active_vs_inactive_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.join_parameterize(:customer, "active_only", active: true)
  |> Selecto.join_parameterize(:customer, "inactive_only", active: false)
  |> Selecto.select([
    "order_number",
    "status",
    "customer:active_only.name",
    "customer:inactive_only.name"
  ])
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JoinLab.Helpers.run("Parameterized joins with boolean filters", active_vs_inactive_query)
```

## 4) Subquery Join (`join_subquery/4`)

Build a derived dataset of high-value delivered orders and join it into a customer query.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

high_value_delivered_orders =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([customer_id, order_number, total]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(status == "delivered" and total > 500))

subquery_join_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.customer_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.join_subquery(:high_value_delivered, high_value_delivered_orders,
    type: :inner,
    on: [%{left: "id", right: "customer_id"}]
  )
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, tier, country, high_value_delivered.order_number, high_value_delivered.total]))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(high_value_delivered.total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JoinLab.Helpers.run("Subquery join for high-value delivered orders", subquery_join_query)
```

## 4.1) Subquery Join with `select_shape/2`

Use `select_shape/2` on a subquery join.
Note: a tuple/list containing only `high_value_delivered.*` fields is interpreted as a subselect node and currently fails for `join_subquery` aliases, so this shape keeps those fields flat and groups them in rendering.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

high_value_delivered_orders_shape =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([customer_id, order_number, total]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(status == "delivered" and total > 500))

subquery_shape_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.customer_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.join_subquery(:high_value_delivered, high_value_delivered_orders_shape,
    type: :inner,
    on: [%{left: "id", right: "customer_id"}]
  )
  |> Selecto.select_shape([
    {"name", "tier", "country"},
    "high_value_delivered.order_number",
    "high_value_delivered.total"
  ])
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(high_value_delivered.total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

{:ok, subquery_shaped_rows} =
  JoinLab.Helpers.run_shape("Subquery join with select_shape", subquery_shape_query)

Enum.each(subquery_shaped_rows, fn [{customer_name, tier, country}, order_number, total] ->
  high_value_order = {order_number, total}

  IO.puts(
    "customer=#{customer_name} tier=#{tier} country=#{country} " <>
      "order=#{elem(high_value_order, 0)} total=#{inspect(elem(high_value_order, 1))}"
  )
end)

:ok
```

## 5) Custom Dynamic Join to an Unconfigured Table

This adds a runtime join to `order_items` under a custom join id (`line_items_custom`) without relying on source associations.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

custom_dynamic_join_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.join(:line_items_custom,
    source: "order_items",
    type: :left,
    owner_key: :id,
    related_key: :order_id,
    fields: %{
      product_id: %{type: :integer},
      quantity: %{type: :integer},
      line_total: %{type: :decimal}
    }
  )
  |> Selecto.select(select([order_number, status, line_items_custom.product_id, line_items_custom.quantity, line_items_custom.line_total]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where((status == "shipped" or status == "delivered") and line_items_custom.quantity >= 2))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(line_items_custom.line_total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(15)

JoinLab.Helpers.run("Custom dynamic join to order_items", custom_dynamic_join_query)
```

## 6) Custom Join Type Swap on Dynamic Join

Switch the previous custom join from `:left` to `:inner` to require matching order items.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

custom_inner_join_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.join(:line_items_custom,
    source: "order_items",
    type: :inner,
    owner_key: :id,
    related_key: :order_id,
    fields: %{
      quantity: %{type: :integer},
      line_total: %{type: :decimal}
    }
  )
  |> Selecto.select(select([order_number, status, line_items_custom.quantity, line_items_custom.line_total]))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(line_items_custom.line_total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JoinLab.Helpers.run("Custom dynamic join with inner type", custom_inner_join_query)
```

## 8) Chain `join_subquery/4` with a CTE

Prepare a CTE for high-value orders, then join a grouped subquery that summarizes
line-item totals by order.

```elixir
line_item_totals_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_items_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select([
    "order_id",
    {:field, {:raw_sql, "SUM(selecto_root.line_total) AS line_total_sum"}, "line_total_sum"}
  ])
  |> Selecto.group_by(group_by([order_id]))

cte_join_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.with_cte("high_value_orders", fn ->
    Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
    |> Selecto.select(select([id, order_number, total]))
    |> Selecto.filter(where(total >= 250))
  end,
    columns: ["id", "order_number", "total"],
    join: [type: :inner, owner_key: :id, related_key: :id, fields: :infer]
  )
  |> Selecto.join_subquery(:line_item_totals, line_item_totals_query,
    type: :left,
    on: [%{left: "id", right: "order_id"}],
    fields: %{line_total_sum: %{type: :decimal}}
  )
  |> Selecto.select(select([order_number, total, line_item_totals.line_total_sum]))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(total)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JoinLab.Helpers.run("CTE plus join_subquery", cte_join_query)
```

## 9) Compare Left and Inner Join Row Counts

This quick benchmark keeps the selected fields identical and changes only the
custom join type.

```elixir
count_join_rows = fn join_type ->
  query =
    Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
    |> Selecto.join(:line_items_compare,
      source: "order_items",
      type: join_type,
      owner_key: :id,
      related_key: :order_id,
      fields: %{id: %{type: :integer}}
    )
    |> Selecto.select(select([id, line_items_compare.id]))

  {micros, {:ok, {rows, _columns, _aliases}}} = :timer.tc(fn -> Selecto.execute(query) end)
  %{join_type: join_type, rows: length(rows), elapsed_ms: Float.round(micros / 1000, 2)}
end

[:left, :inner]
|> Enum.map(count_join_rows)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## 10) Join Troubleshooting Snapshot

When a joined field is missing, inspect registered joins and the generated SQL
before changing the domain.

```elixir
troubleshooting_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([order_number, customer.name, order_items.quantity]))
  |> Selecto.limit(5)

{troubleshooting_sql, troubleshooting_params} = Selecto.to_sql(troubleshooting_query)

%{
  joins: Selecto.joins(troubleshooting_query),
  sql: Selecto.format_sql(troubleshooting_sql),
  params: troubleshooting_params
}
|> IO.inspect(label: "join troubleshooting snapshot", pretty: true)
```

## 11) Join Path Diagram from Query Metadata

Turn `Selecto.joins/1` into a compact Mermaid diagram for review.

```elixir
join_path_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([order_number, customer.name, order_items.quantity]))
  |> Selecto.limit(5)

join_edges =
  Selecto.joins(join_path_query)
  |> Map.keys()
  |> Enum.map(fn join_id ->
    "  orders -->|#{join_id}| #{join_id}"
  end)
  |> Enum.join("\n")

fence = String.duplicate("`", 3)

Kino.Markdown.new("#{fence}mermaid\ngraph LR\n#{join_edges}\n#{fence}")
```

## 12) Ambiguous Fields Across Multiple Custom Joins

When two custom joins expose the same field names, select through explicit join
aliases so the generated SQL remains readable.

```elixir
ambiguous_custom_join_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.join(:all_items,
    source: "order_items",
    type: :left,
    owner_key: :id,
    related_key: :order_id,
    fields: %{quantity: %{type: :integer}, line_total: %{type: :decimal}}
  )
  |> Selecto.join(:bulk_items,
    source: "order_items",
    type: :left,
    owner_key: :id,
    related_key: :order_id,
    fields: %{quantity: %{type: :integer}, line_total: %{type: :decimal}}
  )
  |> Selecto.select(select([order_number, all_items.quantity, bulk_items.quantity, all_items.line_total, bulk_items.line_total]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(bulk_items.quantity >= 2))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JoinLab.Helpers.run("Explicit aliases for ambiguous custom joins", ambiguous_custom_join_query, 10)
```

## 13) Dynamic Join vs Domain-Declared Join

Compare a runtime dynamic join with the domain-declared association path for the
same relationship.

```elixir
domain_declared_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([order_number, order_items.quantity, order_items.line_total]))
  |> Selecto.limit(5)

dynamic_equivalent_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.order_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.join(:runtime_order_items,
    source: "order_items",
    type: :left,
    owner_key: :id,
    related_key: :order_id,
    fields: %{quantity: %{type: :integer}, line_total: %{type: :decimal}}
  )
  |> Selecto.select(select([order_number, runtime_order_items.quantity, runtime_order_items.line_total]))
  |> Selecto.limit(5)

[
  %{style: "domain-declared", sql: Selecto.to_sql(domain_declared_query) |> elem(0) |> Selecto.format_sql()},
  %{style: "dynamic", sql: Selecto.to_sql(dynamic_equivalent_query) |> elem(0) |> Selecto.format_sql()}
]
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## 14) Join Graph with Key Labels

Include owner and related keys in the join graph so relationship direction is
visible.

```elixir
join_graph_rows =
  Selecto.joins(domain_declared_query)
  |> Enum.map(fn {join_id, join_info} ->
    join_config = join_info.config
    on = List.first(join_config.on || [%{}])

    %{
      join: join_id,
      source: join_config.source,
      owner_key: on.left,
      related_key: on.right,
      mermaid: "orders -->|#{on.left} to #{on.right}| #{join_id}"
    }
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new(join_graph_rows)
```

## 15) Duplicate Runtime Join Detection

Detect runtime joins that point at the same source and key pair.

```elixir
runtime_join_specs = [
  %{id: :items_a, source: "order_items", owner_key: :id, related_key: :order_id},
  %{id: :items_b, source: "order_items", owner_key: :id, related_key: :order_id},
  %{id: :customers, source: "customers", owner_key: :customer_id, related_key: :id}
]

runtime_join_specs
|> Enum.group_by(fn spec -> {spec.source, spec.owner_key, spec.related_key} end)
|> Enum.flat_map(fn {_signature, specs} ->
  if length(specs) > 1 do
    Enum.map(specs, &Map.put(&1, :duplicate?, true))
  else
    Enum.map(specs, &Map.put(&1, :duplicate?, false))
  end
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## 16) Promote Dynamic Joins into Domain Metadata

Use this checklist when a dynamic join appears in multiple notebooks or app
views.

```elixir
[
  %{signal: "same source/key pair appears repeatedly", action: "promote to domain join"},
  %{signal: "join fields need labels or formats", action: "promote to domain join"},
  %{signal: "one-off diagnostic query", action: "keep as dynamic join"}
]
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

